  {
   "id": "syn-pltr-2",
   "text": "Shares of enterprise software companies fell on Wednesday after a widely followed analyst report questioned how quickly businesses would see returns on their spending on artificial intelligence tools.\n\nPalantir Technologies was among the biggest decliners, giving back part of a large gain this year. The report argued that many pilot projects had not yet turned into paid deployments and that budgets for next year were being set cautiously.\n\nPalantir has said its own commercial pipeline is strong, and recently reported accelerating growth in the United States. An analyst who follows the company said the sell-off reflected its valuation more than any change in its business, but added that competitive pressure from larger software vendors adding AI features is increasing.\n\nThe broader market was little changed.",
   "context": "article",
   "source": {
    "title": "Software stocks fall as investors question AI spending returns",
    "license": "written by thinkingtext, illustrative (not a news report)"
